From: filnix <filnix@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Share the cells goal_expansion pastes into the clause

goal_expansion_() parses the expanded goal with a sub-parser, copies
the sub-parser's cells into the clause with memcpy, then destroys the
sub-parser. parser_destroy() unshares every cell of the sub-parser's
clause, so a refcounted cell (a bigint, rational or strbuf) in the
expansion drops its last reference and is freed while the clause still
points at it. The clause later reads and writes freed memory.

CLP(Z) goal expansion is enough to hit it: a clause body goal over a
ground integer of at least 2^63, which is a bigint, such as

    :- use_module(library(clpz)).
    m :- Z #= 2^63, write(Z), nl.
    :- initialization((m, halt)).

segfaults, and AddressSanitizer reports a heap-use-after-free in
share_cell_() from set_var(), on memory allocated by parse_number()
under goal_expansion_() and freed by its parser_destroy(). 2^62 fits
in an unmanaged small integer cell, so it does not fail.

Take a reference on the pasted cells, the counterpart of the
unshare_cells() that releases the snipped old goal just above.
---
diff --git a/src/parser.c b/src/parser.c
index b10f0a56..7cef852a 100644
--- a/src/parser.c
+++ b/src/parser.c
@@ -3155,6 +3155,7 @@ static cell *goal_expansion_(parser *p, cell *goal)
 	// paste the new goal...
 
 	memcpy(goal, p2->cl->cells, sizeof(cell)*new_cells);
+	share_cells(goal, new_cells);
 	p->cl->cidx += new_cells;
 
 	//DUMP_TERM("new", p->cl->cells, 0, 0);
